RISC-V: struct fields read from parameter registers, struct pieces and address modes keep their width.

A 32-bit field of a struct that arrives in a 64-bit register is read through a cast, as any int that
comes from a wider value is: an int lives sign-extended in its register, and division, checked
arithmetic and indexing read the whole register.

An address mode folds a constant out of a nested addition only when that addition has the width of
the address, so that a narrower one keeps wrapping at its own width.

Without unaligned access (JitNoUnalignedAccess) the register pieces of a struct argument loaded from
memory carry the alignment of the struct and the flag of the load they come from, and the pieces
copied to the outgoing argument area are no wider than the source is known to be aligned for.

genTotalFrameSize accepts an empty frame.

Regression test: bflat-ts:integers/int_fields_in_registers, bflat-ts:alignment/struct_values_unaligned, bflat-ts:calls/frame_shapes

